exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-ab 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $69,000, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $85,000. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $375,000 up, the line is straight: you keep 53% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 59.65% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%, for the same Employment Insurance reason.
From about $3,000 the Canada workers benefit is bigger than what you pay, so the average is below zero. It is lowest, about -10.04%, at $11,000, and turns positive at about $22,000.
The average passes 10% at $32,000, 20% at $57,000, 30% at $182,000 and 40% at $480,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 40.35%, while the top marginal rate is 47%. It reaches half of its $500,000 value at $59,000.

marginalRate
Up to $2,000 the rate is 0%: Employment Insurance is refunded when you file. Earn a dollar more and you lose that refund, about $33 in all, so the rate spikes just past $2,000.
Then, from $3,000 to about $11,100, the rate is below zero. The Canada workers benefit is paid when you file. For a low salary it builds up by 21 cents for every dollar earned above $2,760, to at most about $1,785. Counting that benefit, each extra dollar of salary leaves you with more than a dollar, so the rate is below zero.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.6 cents of each dollar. The rate steps down where they stop: Employment Insurance at about $69,000, the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600 and its second tier at $85,000.
From about $11,100 to $19,100 the rate is 7.58%: only Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. It is 20.52% from $19,200, when federal tax starts, and about 27.91% from $24,500, when Alberta’s 8% tax starts.
From $27,000 to $38,900 the rate is 42.76%, because the Canada workers benefit shrinks by 15 cents for every dollar above $27,000 (the sweep’s start), on top of 28 cents of Alberta and federal tax. It drops back to 27.92% at $39,000, below its level at $70,000 (35.06%).
Alberta has no tax reduction or premium that bends the line, so there are no spikes. After the pension and insurance steps the rate only climbs: 34.35% at $59,100 (the federal 20.5% bracket), 36.34% from $62,000, then 38%, 41.3%, 42.3%, 43.29%, 47% from $260,000 and 48% from $375,000, where the line goes straight.

yearTotal
This chart adds income tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, then takes off benefits paid to you when you file. At low salaries the total is below zero. It is $15,893 at $70,000, and $25,542 at $100,000.
The line bends upward at each new bracket, then goes straight. From $375,000 up, every extra dollar loses the top rate, 47%, so each extra $10,000 of salary adds $4,700. At $500,000 the total is $201,745."
`;

exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-bc 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $69,000, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $85,000. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $270,000 up, the line is straight: you keep 46.5% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 56.09% of your salary.

averageRate
Your BC sales tax credit is paid from the first dollar, so the average is below zero at every salary up to about $22,000. It is lowest, about -13.57%, at $9,000.
The average passes 10% at $32,000, 20% at $58,000, 30% at $173,000 and 40% at $360,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 43.91%, while the top marginal rate is 53.5%. It reaches half of its $500,000 value at $68,000.

marginalRate
Employment Insurance is refunded when you file if you earn $2,000 or less. Earn a dollar more and you lose that refund, about $33 in all, so the rate spikes just past $2,000.
Then, from $3,000 to about $9,000, the rate is below zero. The Canada workers benefit is paid when you file. For a low salary it builds up by 27 cents for every dollar earned above $3,000, to at most about $1,700. Counting that benefit, each extra dollar of salary leaves you with more than a dollar, so the rate is below zero.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.6 cents of each dollar. The rate steps down where they stop: Employment Insurance at about $69,000, the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600 and its second tier at $85,000.
From about $9,100 to $19,100 the rate is 7.58% to 9.56%: Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, plus 2 cents more for every dollar from $15,200 to $18,800, because the BC sales tax credit, which is paid when you file, shrinks by 2 cents for every dollar above $15,000. It is 20.52% from $19,200, when federal tax starts.
The BC tax reduction shrinks by 3.56 cents for every dollar of net income above $25,020, which lifts the rate to 29.22% from $26,800 (BC’s 2026 base and amount come from the engine; the form we hold is 2025). From $27,700 to $38,800 the rate is 44.07%, higher than at $70,000 (32.91%), because the Canada workers benefit shrinks too. It is 29.23% again from $38,900, until the reduction is gone at about $45,300 of salary.
The rate dips to 25.69% at $45,400, then climbs: 34.22% at $59,100, and the top rate of 53.5% from $270,000, where the line goes straight.

yearTotal
This chart adds income tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, then takes off benefits paid to you when you file. At low salaries the total is below zero. It is $15,652 at $70,000, and $24,627 at $100,000.
The line bends upward at each new bracket, then goes straight. From $270,000 up, every extra dollar loses the top rate, 53.5%, so each extra $10,000 of salary adds $5,350. At $500,000 the total is $219,550."
`;

exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-mb 1`] = `
