Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $69,000, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $85,000. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $405,000 up, the line is straight: you keep 48.74% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 55.57% of your salary.

averageRate
Your Manitoba personal tax credit is paid from the first dollar, so the average is below zero at every salary up to about $20,000. It is lowest, -18.5%, at $1,000, then rises towards zero.
The average passes 10% at $29,000, 20% at $42,000, 30% at $119,000 and 40% at $300,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 44.43%, while the top marginal rate is 51.26%. It reaches half of its $500,000 value at $52,000.

marginalRate
Employment Insurance is refunded when you file if you earn $2,000 or less. Earn a dollar more and you lose that refund, about $33 in all, so the rate spikes just past $2,000.
Then, from $3,000 to about $9,000, the rate is below zero. The Canada workers benefit is paid when you file. For a low salary it builds up by 27 cents for every dollar earned above $3,000, to at most about $1,700. Counting that benefit, each extra dollar of salary leaves you with more than a dollar, so the rate is below zero.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 8.57 cents of each dollar. The rate steps down where they stop: Employment Insurance at about $69,000, the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600 and its second tier at $85,000.
Manitoba’s own tax starts before the federal tax does. From $17,500 the rate is 19.51%: Manitoba tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, and 1 cent more for every dollar, because the Manitoba personal tax credit, which is paid when you file, shrinks by 1 cent for every dollar of income. It is 31.46% from $19,200, once federal tax joins in.
From $27,700 to $38,800 the rate is 45.35%, higher than at $70,000 (37.64%). The Canada workers benefit shrinks by 15 cents for every dollar above $27,700. It falls back to 30.5% at $39,000.
It steps up to 38.88% at $59,100 (the federal 20.5% bracket), then falls as Employment Insurance and the Canada Pension Plan stop: 37.63% at $69,000, 35.92% at $75,000 and 33.25% at $85,000.
At $201,200 of salary it steps up about 1 point to 47.55%, and it reaches 51.26% from $260,000 to $400,000. Then it drops about 1 point from $405,000, where the line goes straight. The cause is the Manitoba basic personal amount. It shrinks to zero as net income goes from $200,000 to $400,000, so you lose a little of the credit on each extra dollar.

yearTotal
This chart adds income tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, then takes off benefits paid to you when you file. At low salaries the total is below zero. It is $18,101 at $70,000, and $28,555 at $100,000.
The line bends upward at each new bracket, then goes straight. From $405,000 up, every extra dollar loses the top rate, 51.26%, so each extra $10,000 of salary adds $5,126. At $500,000 the total is $222,174."
`;

