exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-on 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $69,000, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $85,000. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $260,000 up, the line is straight: you keep 46.47% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 54.62% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%. Employment Insurance is taken from your pay and refunded when you file, so the net is nothing. Just above $2,000 you lose that refund and the average is about 1.63%.
From about $3,200 the Canada workers benefit, which is paid when you file, is bigger than what you pay, so the average is below zero. It is lowest, -12.73%, at $9,000, and turns positive at $21,000.
The average passes 10% at $33,000, 20% at $53,000, 30% at $145,000 and 40% at $305,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 45.38%, while the top marginal rate is 53.53%. It reaches half of its $500,000 value at $68,000.

marginalRate
Up to $2,000 the rate is 0%: Employment Insurance is refunded when you file. Earn a dollar more and you lose that refund, about $33 in all, so the rate spikes just past $2,000.
Then, from $3,000 to about $9,000, the rate is below zero. The Canada workers benefit is paid when you file. For a low salary it builds up by 27 cents for every dollar earned above $3,000, to at most about $1,700. Counting that benefit, each extra dollar of salary leaves you with more than a dollar, so the rate is below zero.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.6 cents of each dollar. The rate steps down where they stop: Employment Insurance at about $69,000, the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600 and its second tier at $85,000.
From about $9,100 to $19,100 the rate is 7.58%: no income tax yet, only Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. It is 20.52% from $19,200, when federal income tax starts.
From about $20,700 to $25,700 of salary the Ontario Health Premium adds 6 cents for every dollar, up to $300 in all.
Between $27,700 and $38,800 of salary the rate is higher than at $70,000 (34.35%). It climbs to 35.37% at $28,000, 44.99% at $33,000 and 50.93% from $36,400, then drops to 30.14%. Three things cause this. The Canada workers benefit, which is paid when you file, shrinks by 15 cents for every dollar you earn above $27,700, and is gone by $38,800. The Ontario Low-income Individuals and Families Tax (LIFT) Credit lowers your Ontario tax. It shrinks by 5 cents for every dollar above $32,500, so more of each extra dollar goes to Ontario tax. The Ontario Health Premium adds 6 cents for every dollar from $36,000 to $38,500 of taxable income. The rate drops when the benefit and the premium step end. LIFT keeps shrinking until $50,000.
Three short spikes push the rate above the top rate of 53.53%: 55% from $48,450 to $49,050, 59.11% from $72,700 to $73,290, and 73.27% from $201,130 to $201,720 of salary. Each one is a step in the Ontario Health Premium. Across a band about $600 wide, the premium grows by 25 cents for every extra dollar, adding $150 in all. The rate falls back as soon as the band ends.
Ontario’s surtax raises the rate twice. A surtax is an extra tax on top of your Ontario tax, charged once that tax passes a set amount. It adds 20 cents for every dollar of Ontario tax above $5,710, and another 36 cents above $7,307. The rate rises to 31.48% at $99,000, and to 37.91% at $116,000.
Above the straight-line start ($260,000 in Ontario) the rate stops rising. Every extra dollar is taxed at 53.53%, so the line is straight.

yearTotal
This chart adds income tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, then takes off benefits paid to you when you file. At low salaries the total is below zero. It is $16,214 at $70,000, and $25,794 at $100,000.
The line bends upward at each new bracket, then goes straight. From $260,000 up, every extra dollar loses the top rate, 53.53%, so each extra $10,000 of salary adds $5,353. At $500,000 the total is $226,922."
`;

